  "question": {
    "text": "A cylinder closed at both ends is separated into two equal parts (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>45</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> each) by a piston impermeable to heat. Both the parts contain the same masses of gas at a temperature of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>300</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math> and a pressure of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi>atm</mi></math>. If now the gas in one of the parts is heated such that the piston shifts by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>, then the temperature and the pressure of the gas in this part after heating is&nbsp;"
  },
